Icel. ''bleikr'' = Sw. ''blek'' = Dan. ''bleg'', pale, wan), < ''blīcan'' (pret. ''blāc'', pp. ''blicen''), shine, = OS. ''blīkan'' = OFries. ''blīka'', shine, = D. ''blijken'' (pret. ''bleek''), appear, = Icel. ''blīkja'', ''blīka'', shine, = OHG. ''blīhhan'', shine (MHG. ''blīcan'', G. ''bleichen'', grow pale, mixed with weak verb ''bleichen'', bleach: see ''bleach''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''), akin to Skt. √ ''bhrāj'', shine, and perhaps to Gr. φλέγειν, burn, blaze, φλόξ, flame, L. ''flamma'', flame, ''fulgere'', shine, etc.: see ''flame'', ''fulgent'', ''phlegm'', ''phlox'', etc. Related E. words are ''blank'', ''blink'', ''bleach''<sup>1</sup>, perhaps ''black'', and ''bright''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1†.''' Pale; pallid; wan; of a sickly hue.
{{smaller block|With a face dedly, ''bleyk'', and pale. {{right|''Lydgate.''}}
She looked as pale and as ''bleak'' as one laid out dead.
{{right|''Foxe'', Martyrs (Agnes Wardall).}}}}
'''2.''' Exposed to cold and winds; desolate; bare of vegetation.
{{smaller block|<poem>Say, will ye bless the ''bleak'' Atlantic shore?</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', Cho. to Brutus.}}
<poem>Wastes too ''bleak'' to rear the common growth of earth.</poem>
{{right|''Wordsworth.''}}
It is rich land, but upon a clay, and in a very ''bleak'', high, exposed situation. {{right|''Gray'', Letters, I. 258.}}}}
'''3.''' Cheerless; dreary.
{{smaller block|Her desolation presents us with nothing but ''bleak'' and barren prospects. {{right|''Addison.''}}}}
'''4.''' Cold; chill; piercing; desolating.
{{smaller block|<poem>Entreat the north
To make his ''bleak'' winds kiss my parched lips.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', K. John, v. 7.}}
The night was ''bleak'' ; the rain fell; the wind roared.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., ix.}}}}

